        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The oracular consultation.</strong> David’s first act after Saul’s death is to inquire of Yahweh — the verb (<em>sha’al</em>) carries cultic technicality, evoking the priestly oracle through the ephod (1 Sam 23:9, 30:7, where Abiathar carries it). The contrast with Saul is structural: Saul’s last act was consulting the medium of Endor (1 Sam 28), having lost access to Yahweh’s word; David opens his reign by re-establishing the legitimate channel. Hebron is chosen not by political calculation but by oracular instruction — the narrative insists that the kingdom is given, not seized. The pattern recurs through 2 Sam 5:19, 5:23: David inquires before each major military move, until ch 11, where he conspicuously does not.",
