        "anointed David king over the house of Judah",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The second anointing.</strong> David is anointed three times: privately by Samuel (1 Sam 16:13), publicly by Judah here, and over all Israel at Hebron (5:3). The three-stage pattern reflects historical realism — kingship in early Israel was built incrementally through tribal acclamation, not imposed by a single act. Hebron, the Calebite stronghold and Abraham’s burial place (Gen 23), gives the southern coronation deep ancestral resonance. David rules seven and a half years from Hebron before relocating north, a span that allowed Judah to consolidate around him while the Saulide dynasty unraveled in the north.",
        "User original",
