        "Mahanaim",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Mahanaim — the rival capital.</strong> Abner relocates Saul’s surviving son across the Jordan to Mahanaim, well outside Philistine reach but also outside the historic Saulide heartland of Benjamin. The choice is a tell: Saul’s territorial base is so compromised by Philistine pressure (the same defeat that killed Saul at Gilboa, 1 Sam 31) that the rump kingdom must shelter in Transjordan. Mahanaim — "two camps" — was the site where Jacob met Esau (Gen 32:2) and would later be David’s own refuge during Absalom’s rebellion (17:24). The narrative is already foreshadowing: the place where the failing king goes to hide will become the place where the successful king flees.',
        "User original",
