    (
        2,
        26,
        "",
        "Shall the sword devour forever?",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Abner’s plea.</strong> One of the great peace-cries in OT narrative: the soldier asking the soldier to call off pursuit before the violence becomes self-sustaining. The phrase "shall the sword devour forever" is theologically loaded — picturing the sword as a personified consumer, never sated, an image picked up by Jeremiah (12:12, 46:10) and embedded in apocalyptic literature. Joab’s grudging assent ("if you had not spoken, surely in the morning the people would have gone away") admits that war by inertia would have continued. The exchange marks the temporary end of phase one of the civil war; phase two will be fought not between armies but between commanders by treachery.',
        "User original",
    ),
