    (
        3,
        2,
        "",
        "Sons were born to David in Hebron",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Hebron sons.</strong> Six sons by six different wives in seven and a half years: a list of future trouble. Amnon (firstborn) will rape Tamar; Absalom will kill Amnon and rebel; Adonijah will attempt the throne against Solomon. The text inserts the genealogy at exactly this point — between Saul’s collapse and David’s consolidation — to show what David is building alongside the kingdom: a polygynous royal harem that will produce the dynastic crises of the rest of the book. Deut 17:17 had specifically forbidden the king to multiply wives; the verse is a quiet narrative indictment that pays out across the next twenty chapters.",
        "User original",
    ),
