    (
        3,
        16,
        "",
        "weeping as he went",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Paltiel’s grief.</strong> One of the OT’s quietest, sharpest character vignettes. Paltiel — given Michal as wife by Saul after David’s flight (1 Sam 25:44) — has lived with her for years and now follows her in tears as she is taken back. Abner’s curt "go, return" cuts off the relationship without ceremony. The text registers his loss without comment, but the placement matters: this is a small private cost of David’s political reconsolidation, set in a chapter dominated by men’s strategic calculations. Whatever Michal’s coldness toward David at 6:20 will mean later, the narrator has already shown us where part of her heart had been.',
        "User original",
    ),
