    (
        3,
        27,
        "",
        "struck him there in the body",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joab kills Abner.</strong> Joab takes Abner aside under pretext of private conversation and stabs him at the gate of Hebron — a city of refuge (Josh 21:13). The illegality is laid on three levels: a treacherous welcome (a violation of guest-friendship), an act outside the rules of blood-vengeance (Asahel was killed in regulated combat, not premeditated murder), and a killing inside a refuge city. David’s curse in 3:29 condemns Joab juridically; his lament (3:33–34) absolves the throne politically; but the narrative will hold the debt open until Solomon settles it (1 Kgs 2:5–6, 28–34). Joab’s competence is indispensable, his methods are unforgivable, and David lacks the political strength to remove him — the contradiction will run the rest of the book.",
        "User original",
    ),
