        3,
        33,
        "",
        "Should Abner die as a fool dies?",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>David’s lament for Abner.</strong> A short, formal couplet preserved as poetry: "Should Abner die as a fool dies? Your hands were not bound, your feet not put in fetters; as one falls before the wicked, so you fell." The poem performs three political functions at once: honoring the slain commander (essential for winning over Israel), distancing David from Joab’s act ("all the people understood that day that it was not of the king to kill Abner," 3:37), and signaling that political assassination is not the Davidic style. The lament’s craft — like the lament for Saul and Jonathan (1:19–27) — argues for David as a working poet, not just the patron of court poets, well before the Psalter tradition.',
        "User original",
