        "",
        "David danced before Yahweh with all his might",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>M’karker (<em>מְכַרְכֵּר</em>).</strong> A rare verb (only here and 6:16) — likely intensive whirling or leaping. David is described as wearing only a linen ephod (a priestly garment), an extraordinary embodiment of priestly-royal fusion. Michal’s contempt (6:20–23) and David’s defiant response ("I will be even more contemptible than this," 6:22) mark the moment Saul’s daughter is finally cut off from the Davidic future — and from any further child-bearing role (6:23). The chapter records both the high point of David’s religious devotion and the final closure of the Saulide line through Michal.',
        "User original",
