    (
        7,
        13,
        "",
        "I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Davidic Covenant.</strong> The pivot of OT theology: an unconditional dynastic promise. <em>Forever</em> commits Yahweh to a perpetual Davidic line. When the line is interrupted at the exile, the promise becomes the engine of messianic expectation — a future Davidic king will fulfill what the historical kings failed to embody. Heb 1:5 and Acts 2:30 cite this verse as messianic; the NT places Jesus in the Davidic line precisely to claim its fulfillment. Modern scholarship reads the covenant as foundational to both Jewish messianism and Christian christology.",
        "User original",
    ),
