        14,
        "",
        "I will be his father, and he will be my son",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The adoption formula.</strong> Royal adoption language drawn from ancient Near Eastern coronation ceremonies — see Ps 2:7 ("you are my son; today I have begotten you"). In its OT context the formula refers to Solomon and the Davidic line collectively; in NT use (Heb 1:5) it is read messianically. The verse anchors the long Christian theological development of divine sonship. Note the conditional clause that immediately follows: "if he commits iniquity, I will discipline him" — the Davidic promise is unconditional in continuance, but particular kings are accountable.',
        "User original",
