    (
        8,
        15,
        "",
        "executed justice and righteousness",
        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The royal-justice formula.</strong> <em>Mishpat u-tzedakah</em> ("justice and righteousness") is the standard ANE summary of legitimate kingship — the same phrasing recurs in Akkadian royal inscriptions (Hammurabi, the Neo-Assyrian kings) as the boast of every monarch worth the title. The verse caps the chapter’s catalog of foreign conquests with the domestic claim: David doesn’t just expand the realm, he governs it justly. The placement is structural: this is the high-water mark of the Davidic narrative, immediately before the moral collapse of chs 11–12. Brueggemann reads the contrast as the editorial argument of the books of Samuel — kingship can mean either justice-and-righteousness or its catastrophic absence, and the same king embodies both.',
        "User paraphrase; references Hammurabi; summarises Brueggemann",
    ),
