    (
        9,
        1,
        "",
        "kindness for Jonathan",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Ḥesed across generations.</strong> The Hebrew <em>ḥesed</em> — covenant loyalty, often translated "loving-kindness" or "steadfast love" — is the operative word in this chapter, used three times (9:1, 3, 7). David is honoring the covenant he and Jonathan made in 1 Sam 20:14–17, where Jonathan asked David to show <em>ḥesed</em> not just to him but to his descendants forever. Royal practice in the ANE was the opposite: a new king typically eliminated his predecessor’s line to preempt rival claims. David’s deliberate inversion of that practice, eight chapters into his consolidated reign, is one of the few unambiguously generous acts in the book — and the only one initiated by David alone, with no prophet or political pressure prompting it.',
        "User original",
    ),
