    (
        10,
        4,
        "",
        "shaved off one half of their beards",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The shaming of the envoys.</strong> Beards were a primary marker of male honor in the ANE (Lev 19:27, 21:5 protect them from mourning rituals); shaving an enemy’s beard appears as a humiliation rite in Isa 7:20 and 50:6. Hanun’s gesture is calibrated: he doesn’t kill the envoys (which would be a clear act of war) but disgraces them in a way that makes diplomatic patience impossible. David’s order that they wait at Jericho until their beards regrow (10:5) registers the depth of the insult — they cannot be seen in court in this state. The episode is the proximate cause of the war that frames chs 11–12: the siege of Rabbah is on (11:1) precisely because of this provocation.",
        "User original",
    ),
