    (
        16,
        21,
        "",
        "Go in to your father",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Ahithophel’s counsel and Nathan’s oracle.</strong> Ahithophel advises Absalom to publicly take David’s concubines on the palace roof — a calculated political theater (taking the king’s women is a public claim on the throne, cf. 3:7) and a point of no return that would make reconciliation with David impossible. The narrator stages it on the same rooftop where David first saw Bathsheba (11:2), making the symmetry impossible to miss. This is the explicit fulfillment of Nathan’s oracle in 12:11–12: "I will take your wives before your eyes… in the sight of this sun." Ahithophel’s strategic brilliance is, at the level of narrative theology, also Yahweh’s judgment working through human calculation. The political event and the prophetic word converge on the same act.',
        "User original",
    ),
