    (
        17,
        14,
        "",
        "Yahweh had ordained to defeat the good counsel of Ahithophel",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The narrator’s theological aside.</strong> One of the rare moments where the narrator breaks frame to comment on causation. Hushai’s counsel is rhetorically inferior — slower, less decisive, militarily worse — yet Absalom and the elders prefer it. The narrator tells us why: Yahweh has ordained Ahithophel’s defeat. The verse is a textbook case of inner-biblical theology operating through deliberation rather than miracle: divine purpose runs through Absalom’s political instincts, Hushai’s rhetorical skill, and Ahithophel’s vanity simultaneously, without any of them seeing it. The same pattern appears in the Joseph narrative ("you meant evil, but God meant it for good," Gen 50:20) — biblical providence as the secret meaning of free human choices.',
        "User original",
    ),
