    (
        19,
        5,
        "",
        "Today you have shamed the faces of all your servants",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joab’s rebuke.</strong> Joab forces the political reality back on David: a king who publicly mourns the rebel son and ignores the loyal soldiers who saved his throne risks losing them. The rebuke is brutal but accurate, and David obeys it. The exchange completes a portrait the book has been building: Joab as the king’s indispensable corrective, the man who keeps David functional when grief or scruple threatens the regime. The relationship is the structural inverse of David and Nathan — the prophet rebukes the king on God’s behalf, Joab rebukes the king on the kingdom’s behalf. Both kinds of speaking truth to power are necessary; both leave their speakers enemies of the throne in the end (cf. Joab’s execution by Solomon, 1 Kgs 2:28–34).",
        "User original",
    ),
