        21,
        10,
        "",
        "Rizpah the daughter of Aiah took sackcloth",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Rizpah’s vigil.</strong> One of the most unsparing scenes in the Hebrew Bible. The same Rizpah whose name first appeared as Saul’s concubine (3:7) — defined then only by her political utility — now keeps watch over the bodies of her two sons and five other Saulides hung up to appease the Gibeonites, fending off birds and jackals from "the beginning of harvest until water poured on them from the sky" (roughly April through October, Israel’s dry season). David, on hearing of her vigil, finally retrieves and buries the remains (21:12–14). The text gives no direct moral verdict on either the executions or her vigil, but the structure speaks: a grieving mother’s persistence shames a king into giving his predecessor’s line proper burial. Phyllis Trible and others have read Rizpah as one of scripture’s great witnesses to the moral weight of bodies.',
        "User original",
