    (
        21,
        19,
        "",
        "Elhanan the son of Jaare-Oregim the Bethlehemite killed Goliath",
        "source",
        "Variant",
        "Variant",
        '<strong>Who killed Goliath?</strong> 2 Sam 21:19 says Elhanan killed Goliath the Gittite, in flat contradiction with 1 Sam 17 (David and Goliath). 1 Chr 20:5, telling the same story as 2 Sam 21:19, harmonizes by reading "Elhanan… killed Lahmi the brother of Goliath." Most modern textual critics (e.g., McCarter, Anchor Bible) treat the Chronicler’s text as a deliberate harmonization and 2 Sam 21:19 as preserving an older tradition in which Elhanan was the original Goliath-slayer; the David-Goliath story of 1 Sam 17 is then read as a transferred or composite tradition. The traditional alternative — that two different giants happened to share the name — is defensible but less economical. The verse is one of the textbook cases where two biblical narratives cannot both be straightforwardly historical without harmonization.',
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 11 ----
    (
        11,
        1,
        "",
        "the time when kings go out",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"When kings go out to battle."</strong> The narrator’s setup: "but David remained at Jerusalem." A single sentence frames the entire Bathsheba narrative — David’s absence from his proper place creates the space for the catastrophe that follows. Spring (Apr–May) was the standard ANE campaign season, when winter rains had ended but summer heat had not yet set in. The Joab-led siege of Rabbah will produce Uriah’s death; the king’s palace produces David’s adultery. The chapter’s opening verse is one of the most quietly damning sentences in scripture.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        11,
        27,
        "",
        "the thing that David had done displeased Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The narrator’s judgment.</strong> The first explicit moral verdict on David. Until this verse, the narrator has shown the events; here, for the first and decisively, he comments. The Bathsheba/Uriah affair becomes the wound in the Davidic narrative that never fully heals — every subsequent calamity (Amnon’s rape of Tamar, Absalom’s rebellion, the Sheba revolt) is traced back to it through the Nathan oracle of ch 12. Few biblical narratives are as morally surgical.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 12 ----
    (
        12,
        7,
        "",
        "You are the man",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Nathan’s parable.</strong> One of the great moral set-pieces of scripture. Nathan tells David a parable of a rich man who steals a poor man’s ewe lamb; David, outraged, demands judgment; Nathan turns the parable on him. The structure — get the audience to convict themselves before revealing the indictment — is a powerful rhetorical form, used again by Joab via the wise woman of Tekoa in 14:1–20, and by Jesus in several parables (the two debtors, Luke 7:41–47). Few prophetic confrontations are as economically devastating.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        12,
        13,
        "",
        "I have sinned against Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>David’s confession.</strong> The traditional superscription of Psalm 51 ("when Nathan the prophet came to him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba") attaches that great penitential psalm to this moment. The brevity of the confession here — five Hebrew words, <em>ḥata’ti laYHWH</em> — contrasts with the elaborate structure of the psalm and with the lengthy Saulite excuse-making (1 Sam 15). The Davidic narrative gives us the principle: the great king is the one who admits the sin without qualification.',
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 13 ----
    (
        13,
        1,
        "",
        "Tamar",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Tamar’s rape.</strong> Phyllis Trible’s reading in <em>Texts of Terror</em> remains influential: the narrative refuses to look away from the violence done to Tamar, and its placement immediately after Nathan’s "the sword shall never depart from your house" oracle (12:10) presents Amnon’s crime as the first installment of the Davidic curse. David’s silent fury (13:21 — "but he did nothing") becomes the moral and political vacuum that Absalom fills. The chapter is one of the OT’s starkest portraits of paternal failure following royal sin.',
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 14 ----
    (
        14,
        2,
        "",
        "a  wise woman",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The wise woman of Tekoa.</strong> Joab arranges for a parable-bearer in the same shape as Nathan’s — get the king to render judgment in a fictional case, then apply it to himself. The "wise woman" (<em>’ishah ḥakhamah</em>) is a recognized social role in ancient Israel (compare 2 Sam 20:16 — the wise woman of Abel). The chapter shows that the prophetic-parable form is not unique to prophets but a known mode of speaking truth to royal power. The pattern recurs through Hebrew narrative.',
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 15 ----
    (
        15,
        30,
        "",
        "David went up by the ascent of the Mount of Olives",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>David flees Jerusalem.</strong> The king crosses the Kidron, ascends the Mount of Olives barefoot and weeping. The geography and posture become typologically significant: Jesus will cross the same Kidron and weep on the same Mount of Olives in Luke 19, Matt 26, John 18 — frequently read by patristic and medieval interpreters as deliberate Davidic typology. The Absalom rebellion is the structural inverse of David’s rise: the king now flees rather than ascends, the people he led now follow another, his counselor Ahithophel now sides with his son.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- ch 18 ----
    (
        18,
        33,
        "",
        "My son Absalom! My son, my son Absalom!",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"O my son Absalom."</strong> One of the most quoted laments in literature: "would I had died for you, O Absalom, my son, my son!" David grieves the rebel son who tried to kill him. Joab’s rebuke in 19:5–7 — your soldiers risked their lives for you; you’re shaming their victory by mourning the man who would have destroyed them — is the political counterpoint to David’s paternal grief. The text holds both honestly: the king must function, the father cannot stop weeping.',
        "User original",
    ),
