    (
        22,
        2,
        "",
        "Yahweh is my rock",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>David’s psalm.</strong> 2 Sam 22 is preserved nearly verbatim as Psalm 18 — one of the few cases where the same poem appears in two biblical books. Minor textual variations between the two versions are studied by textual critics as a case study in how biblical poetry was transmitted. The placement here — late in David’s life, as a retrospective of deliverance — gives the psalm a different framing than the Psalter’s liturgical context. The dual preservation is rare and theologically significant: the same prayer functions both as David’s personal song and as Israel’s communal liturgy.",
        "User original",
    ),
