    # ---- ch 23 ----
    (
        23,
        1,
        "",
        "last words of David",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"The last words of David."</strong> A poetic oracle (23:2–7) where David speaks as a prophet — "the Spirit of Yahweh spoke through me" (23:2). The poem articulates the ideal of just kingship under God: "one who rules over men righteously, who rules in the fear of God, is as the light of the morning" (23:3–4). After David’s catastrophic moral failures earlier in the book, the ideal is voiced by him as both confession and aspiration. The placement frames the Davidic legacy not by what was achieved but by what was glimpsed.',
        "User original",
    ),
