        "",
        "Let us fall now into Yahweh’s hand",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>David’s choice.</strong> Faced with three punishments — famine, military defeat, plague — David chooses plague, "for his mercies are great." The principle is theologically dense: divine punishment is preferable to human/political because mercy attaches to it. The choice frames a recurring biblical theme: better the unmediated discipline of God than the mediated cruelty of human enemies. Compare Heb 10:31 inverting the same image ("fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God").',
        "User original",
