        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>David’s silence.</strong> The MT says only that David was "very angry"; the LXX and 4QSamᵃ preserve a longer reading: "but he would not punish his son Amnon, because he loved him, since he was his firstborn." Most modern translations (NRSV, NABRE) restore the longer reading, which makes explicit what the MT only implies — David’s rage produces no action. The vacuum is what Absalom fills: two years later (13:23) he kills Amnon himself, and the failure of paternal justice becomes the seed of the rebellion. The verse is one of the sharpest places where the David narrative exposes the cost of his moral compromise after the Bathsheba affair: the king who once executed king-killers swiftly (1:15, 4:12) cannot now bring himself to act against his own son. Robert Alter reads it as the narrative’s anatomy of Davidic paralysis.',
