        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Absalom’s hair.</strong> Two hundred shekels is roughly five pounds — implausible as the literal weight of any cut of human hair, almost certainly a hyperbolic detail meant to mark Absalom as exceptionally beautiful (the chapter has just told us, 14:25, that "from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head there was no blemish in him"). The narrative will return to that hair in 18:9, where Absalom’s head is caught in the branches of an oak as he flees the battle, leaving him hanging while his mule rides on. The most-prized feature becomes the instrument of his death — a deliberate literary closure of the kind Hebrew narrative reserves for figures whose vanity defines them. The text invites no allegory but builds the link unmistakably: what made Absalom dangerous is what kills him.',
        "User original",
