        21,
        "",
        "whether for death or for life, your servant will be there also",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Ittai’s oath.</strong> Ittai the Gittite — a Philistine mercenary commander who arrived in David’s service only "yesterday" (15:20) — pledges loyalty to the fleeing king with language that consciously echoes Ruth’s oath to Naomi (Ruth 1:16–17). The two passages are the OT’s great covenantal oaths spoken by foreigners to Israelites at moments of catastrophe, and both reverse the usual direction of fidelity: the outsider out-loyals the insider. Ittai is one of three Gathite commanders David trusts (cf. 18:2), and the inclusion of a Philistine — historically the archetypal enemy of Israel — at the head of David’s troops in his hour of crisis is part of the chapter’s argument: those who follow David in his weakness are not necessarily those who would have followed him in his strength.',
        "User original",
