        31,
        "",
        "turn the counsel of Ahithophel into foolishness",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The prayer that the narrative answers.</strong> David’s short prayer here is precisely fulfilled in 17:14: "Yahweh had ordained to defeat the good counsel of Ahithophel." Few biblical prayers are answered with such structural exactness — the request is granted, by name, two chapters later. The narrative pattern instructs the reader on how the book’s providence works: Yahweh acts through Hushai’s rhetorical skill and Absalom’s political vanity rather than by miracle, but the connection between the petition and its answer is unmistakable. Compare the way Hannah’s prayer (1 Sam 1:11) and Solomon’s (1 Kgs 3:9) operate in the same narrative-providence mode — explicit request, deferred and indirect fulfillment, narrator’s comment confirming the linkage. The books of Samuel teach the reader to recognize these arcs.',
