        "rode on a cherub, and flew",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The cherub-throne theophany.</strong> The image — Yahweh riding on a cherub, flying on the wings of the wind — draws on ANE storm-god iconography, particularly the Canaanite Baal-Hadad, who likewise rides clouds and storms. The same imagery underlies Ps 18:10 (the parallel preservation of this poem) and is developed dramatically in Ezek 1 and 10 (the cherub-throne chariot of the divine glory). The cherubim themselves are not the chubby winged children of post-Renaissance art but composite guardian-creatures of the ANE imagination — winged, lion-or-bull-bodied, often human-faced (the Akkadian <em>kāribu</em>). The verse is one of the OT’s clearest cases of the inspired writer absorbing imagery from surrounding cultures and re-deploying it: the storm-god vocabulary is now spoken of Yahweh, the chariot-throne is now Yahweh’s, the foreign visual world has been theologically annexed.",
