        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>Ḥasid (<em>חָסִיד</em>).</strong> "With the merciful you will show yourself merciful" — the Hebrew root is <em>ḥesed</em>, the same word that runs through ch 9 (David’s kindness to Mephibosheth). The <em>ḥasid</em> is one bound by covenant loyalty, the human partner who returns Yahweh’s <em>ḥesed</em> with reciprocal faithfulness. The word later becomes the name of a Jewish religious movement (the Ḥasidim of the Maccabean era, then the Ḥasidic Judaism of the eighteenth century). The verse opens a four-fold meditation (22:26–27) on the principle that Yahweh’s response mirrors human disposition — a theology developed in Ps 18:25–26 (the parallel) and quietly contested by Job, who finds it inadequate to his case. The verse is both a confession and a question.',
