        '<strong>David’s lament.</strong> The most quoted expression of paternal grief in literature. Five times David repeats his son’s name; the Hebrew is even more devastating in its rhythm: <em>b’ni Avshalom, b’ni b’ni Avshalom, mi yiten muti ani taḥtekha, Avshalom, b’ni, b’ni</em>. "Would I had died instead of you" — the echo of Christian substitutionary atonement is unmistakable, and Christian commentators from Augustine onward have read the verse typologically.',
